{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head f953c04cfbda44ed2da170038e1b2a5652956ad4, all 35 artifacts, Git README first.\n\nP2: Recover a complete staging file when the final append applied but its answer was lost before hashing (packages/git/src/publisher/gitops.ts:504-518; packages/log/src/publisher.ts:240). stageLog appends bytes, then separately hashes/stores the complete object. If the append's exec reply is lost after applying, its catch returns failure while the full raw file survives. Every later probe reports this object missing with have===size. stageAll computes take=0, sends no part and stops for no progress; stageLog hashes only in the parts loop, so the complete object is never finalized. Independent real-Git fixture runs the actual shell append and then drops precisely that exec answer. The initial publisher exhausts its attempts with unresolved; a reopened LogPublisher and newly created GitOps on the same still-running sandbox repeat unresolved. The canonical ref remains its prior commit. This is a persistent publication deadlock after an ordinary lost answer, not an uncertain ref guess.\n\nOn probe/recovery, finalize any complete staged file for that cohort using its expected type/size/SHA. Confirm storage and exact object identity before declaring it present; discard or restart corrupt complete data conservatively. Make finalization itself idempotent across a lost hash-object reply, failed cleanup, and a DO restart with the container's filesystem retained. Add regressions for the lost final-append reply, process interruption between append and hashing, and corrupted/failed finalization. Preserve the fixed cohort and ref lease; do not require callers to choose a different cohort or erase the active publication to recover.\n\nArchitecture: Bounded per-call staging fixes the transfer-size design issue and permits a single object larger than the transfer bound. Existing 64MiB-plus MemoryGit and actual default 8MiB-plus GitOps/LogPublisher